      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright 2000 S. Karger AG, Basel.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Eosinophils play an important role in asthmatic airway inflammation. Monoclonal antibody EG2 has been considered to identify activated eosinophils."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "The present study was aimed to investigate whether immunohistochemically stained EG2+ eosinophils in sputum reflect the severity of asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Sputum was obtained in 23 asthmatic patients, of whom 13 patients were examined before and after antiasthma treatment including steroid preparations. We used immunohistochemical staining to detect EG2+ (activation marker) eosinophils and fluoroimmunoassay to detect eosinophil cationic protein (ECP)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Moderate to severe asthmatics had a significantly higher proportion of eosinophils and EG2+ eosinophils and higher levels of ECP compared to mild asthmatics (40.9 +/- 5.8 vs. 6.4 +/- 1.2%, 35.5 +/- 5.6 vs. 2.7 +/- 1.0%, 1.470.2 +/- 251.5 vs. 210.6 +/- 52.0 microgram/l, respectively; p < 0.01). Significant increases in proportions of eosinophils, EG2+ eosinophils and ECP in the sputum from patients with exacerbated asthma were evident. The proportions of eosinophils, EG2+ eosinophils, and the levels of ECP were reduced following treatment with antiasthmatic drugs. FEV(1) and FEV(1)/FVC were significantly correlated with EG2+ eosinophils."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "These findings demonstrate that EG2+ eosinophils in sputum are closely related to the clinical status in patients with asthma."
          }
        ]
      },
